mbda,$ then we write $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda).$\n\nFor example, suppose that calls come in at an average rate of $3$ calls per minute at a call center. Then the number of calls $X$ that are received during the next minute has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3^x e^{-3}}{x!}\n$$\n\nIn particular, the probability of receiving $4$ calls during the next minute is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4) & =f(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{4}{e}^{-3}}{4!} \\\\  & ≈0.168\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158491 #edb/ref 1065 4525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158491 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6e07ef85-f3ca-52c8-89e2-062d8ea7c9eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158491 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158493 #edb/ref 1064 "The shape of the graph of the Poisson distribution depends on the value of $\\lambda.$ When $\\lambda$ is small, the probability is concentrated at small values of $x.$ When $\\lambda$ is larger, the probability is concentrated at larger values of $x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8af371733bd248a4e5ba3bc35199f00414188e9ca033a04dee2a7de4b0a7db4.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158493 #edb/ref 1065 4534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158493 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f0026198-16d4-5332-9f5b-036bd00ef32d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158493 #edb/ref 1067 "The Graph of the Poisson Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158786 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186158787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158786 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186158788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158786 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186158789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158786 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186158790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158786 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186158791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158786 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158786 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921861